# Shop Builder: Block customization

This is where a working storefront becomes a good one: the copy, imagery, section
organization, and pricing presentation inside each block. Same `update-block` mechanics
throughout; the craft is in the choices.

## How to think: use customization to convert

- **Copy in the brand voice, concrete and buyer-focused.** Answer the buyer's real question.
  Use rich-text hierarchy on purpose: a hero headline as `<h1>`, a subtitle as `<p>`, so the
  eye lands where you want.
- **Store sections are merchandising inside the store.** Group by intent. Put high-value bundles
  near the top for price anchoring. Order sections by what converts. Match card layout to
  content: `featured` for bundles and currency packs, vertical grids for cosmetics. Section
  titles should sell, not just label.
- **Imagery is consistency plus legibility.** Use on-brand art in one style, an image on every
  card, and backgrounds that set mood without beating the content. Darken a busy background with
  a gradient, never with an opaque color tint.
- **Pricing and trust close the sale.** Rely on regional pricing so locals see local money.
  Surface payment methods, a "cosmetic only / not pay-to-win" reassurance where true, and a
  support contact.

## How to build

### The patch model

`update-block --data '{"r1":{"type":"<t>","id":"<id>","patches":[...]}}'`. Target `block`,
`page`, or `site`. Block patches address paths under `values` (for example
`["values","title"]`); a path missing the `values` prefix returns ok and changes nothing.

**The one exception — `components`.** Store sections and FAQ questions do not live under
`values`; they live in `block.components[]`, a sibling of `values` at the block root. Patch
them at path `["components"]` — no `values` prefix. This is the explicit exception to the rule
above: `["components"]` takes effect, while the intuitive `["values","components"]` is a silent
no-op. Every `update-block` returns `ok:true` either way, so the wrong path fails silently.

### Text (rich HTML)

Set a `localized-value-descriptor` whose `localizedString` values are HTML, one per locale:
```
{"op":"replace","path":["values","title"],"value":{
  "__type":"localized-value-descriptor","enable":true,
  "localizedString":{"en-US":"<h1>Hold the Line</h1>","fr-FR":"<h1>Tenez la ligne</h1>"}}}
```
Wrap body copy in `<p>`, hero headlines in `<h1>`. Bare strings render in a flat plain-text
style. `get-structure` will not show the text back (it returns `L:` ids); confirm via the
`update-block` response's `localizations` map.

### Images

Upload once, reference the returned CDN URL:
```
xsolla shopbuilder upload-asset --merchant-id <m> --project-id <p> --landing-id <landing> \
  --type image --file <path> --json
```
For native blocks (hero logo, hero background), set the URL on the block's image field. For
federated blocks (offer chain), the image lives in
`values.resources.mediaValues["I:..."].src`; repoint that entry's `src` and darken via its
`gradient`.

### Backgrounds and the tint trap

`background.color` renders as a tint over the image. An opaque color hides the image. Keep the
color transparent or low-alpha and do the darkening with `background.gradient` (for a hero,
fade light at the top to solid at the bottom so it blends into the page background).

### Store sections (the `newStore` block's `components[]`)

Sections live in the store block's `components[]` — at the **block root**, a sibling of
`values`, not inside it. Patch the whole array at `["components"]` (the patch-model exception
above); there is no move op for components, so reorder by rewriting the array.

**A fresh `newStore` block renders Xsolla demo data until you replace its placeholder.** The
default `components[0]` has `section.item.group: "__test__/bundle"`, which renders "Mystic Kit"
/ "Test item" / a single "Bundles" tab. `ok:true` from `update-block` and a healthy
`get-structure` do **not** mean the real catalog is showing — the block existing is not the
block being wired.

Fix: read the block, keep the placeholder's `card.layouts` object (it holds every layout-type
definition), and replace the whole array with real sections:
```
xsolla shopbuilder update-block --merchant-id <m> --project-id <p> --landing-id <landing> \
  --data '{"r1":{"type":"block","id":"<blockId>","patches":[
    {"op":"replace","path":["components"],"value":[<sections>]}]}}' --json
```
Each section is a `newStoreSection`:
```
{"_id":"<id>","type":"newStoreSection","enable":true,
 "card":{"layouts":{ …copy verbatim from the placeholder… },"selectedLayoutType":"vertical"},
 "section":{"item":{"group":"<group>","type":"<type>","autoSelected":false},
            "title":<LVD>,"background":<…>,"hiddenEmpty":true,"horizontalScroll":false}}
```
Copy `card.layouts` verbatim from the existing placeholder section (it defines all layout
types); only `selectedLayoutType` changes per section. Section item `type`:
- `virtual_currency` with group `__all__` shows the currency packages (top-up). Packages are
  not shown by group or bundle type.
- `virtual_good` shows virtual items (skins, gear, boosters, passes) by group.
- `bundle` shows bundles by group.

**Layout — `featured` is a single-item carousel.** It shows one item at a time with pagination
dots and hides the rest: good as a hero/anchor, wrong where the buyer needs to compare (currency
tiers, a full skins grid). For those, use a grid layout like `vertical` so all items show at
once. A `featured` section reads as "the store only loaded one item" — it didn't; that's the
carousel.

### FAQ questions (the `faq` block's `components[]`)

FAQ questions are also `components[]` at the block root, each a `questionV2`:
```
{"_id":"<id>","enable":true,"type":"questionV2","question":<LVD>,"answer":<LVD>,"value":{ …image }}
```
Patch at `["components"]`. Writing `values.questions` returns `ok:true` and silently does
nothing — the same trap as store sections.

### Per-block and site theme

Theme fields live under `theme`: `mainColors` (accentColor, secondaryColor, textColor,
buttonTextColor, overlayColor, borderColor), `buttonBorderRadius`, `backgroundBlur`, `fonts`,
`pictureBackground`. Set them on the page (the look that ships) and mirror on the site.

## Common pitfalls

- Block patch paths without the `values` prefix; silent no-op. Exception: `components` (see
  the patch model) is patched at `["components"]`, block root.
- Patching `["values","components"]` for sections or `values.questions` for FAQ; both return
  `ok:true` and do nothing. Sections and questions live at `["components"]` (block root).
- Trusting `ok:true` + `get-structure` as proof the store is wired. A fresh block shows demo
  data ("Mystic Kit") until `components[0]`'s `__test__/...` section is replaced. Render it.
- Reading a `featured` section as "only one item loaded"; it is a single-item carousel by
  design. Use `vertical` (grid) where items must be compared.
- Plain-string text; renders unstyled. Always HTML.
- Opaque `background.color`; hides the image. Darken with the gradient.
- Currency packages placed in a `bundle` or grouped section; they return empty. Use a
  `virtual_currency` section with group `__all__`.

## Verify

- The `update-block` response `localizations` map contains your HTML text.
- `get-structure` shows each section's `group`, `type`, and layout, and image `src` values set,
  and no section still points at a `__test__/...` group.
- Read the client catalog calls to confirm each section resolves to real items.
- **Render the store, don't just check structure.** `get-structure` passing while every section
  is still demo data is exactly the failure this skill guards against. Load the authenticated
  preview and eyeball it (see shopbuilder-storefront, "Render to verify").
